-- DICT-SEMANTICS §3 `assum`-BEFORE-`inst` PRECEDENCE, CROSS-MODULE. #2548 asks
-- by name for a gate on this precedence itself: "There is no gate for the
-- `assum`-before-`inst` precedence itself, and one must exist before this
-- lands... a grader written afterwards can only confirm whatever the new code
-- does." Every EXISTING assum/inst precedence row in this file
-- (`s3-nested-obligation-most-specific`, `s6-1-4-*`, `s-nary-given-*`) is
-- SINGLE-MODULE; this is the first CROSS-MODULE one. LAYOUT:
--   iface.mdk -- interface Tag2, impl Tag2 Int, impl Tag2 Bool
--   mix.mdk   -- mix : Tag2 a => a -> Int; mix x = tagn x + tagn (True : Bool)
--   main.mdk  -- calls mix at two different ground types
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, hand-derived from DICT-SEMANTICS.md §3's precedence paragraph
-- BEFORE running anything:
--
--   `mix (5 : Int)`: instantiates `mix`'s own `a := Int`, so the caller's
--   dict is for `Tag2 Int`.
--     `tagn x`              -- goal `Tag2 a` (literally the declared
--       predicate at THIS instantiation, `Tag2 Int`) -- `assum` DISCHARGES
--       IT FROM THE PASSED DICT, giving `impl Tag2 Int`'s body: 1.
--     `tagn (True : Bool)`  -- goal `Tag2 Bool`, structurally distinct from
--       the assumption `Tag2 a` -- `assum` stays silent, `inst` commits to
--       `impl Tag2 Bool`: 2.
--   Total: 1 + 2 = 3.
--
--   `mix (True : Bool)`: `a := Bool`, caller's dict is for `Tag2 Bool`.
--     `tagn x`              -- goal `Tag2 a` = `Tag2 Bool` at this
--       instantiation -- `assum` again, forwarding the SAME dict `inst` would
--       have built independently at `impl Tag2 Bool`: 2.
--     `tagn (True : Bool)`  -- unchanged from above, still `inst` at
--       `impl Tag2 Bool`: 2.
--   Total: 2 + 2 = 4.
--
-- The wrong answer this row would catch: if `assum` were skipped and BOTH
-- goals fell to `inst`, the first call would still print 3 (inst also finds
-- `impl Tag2 Int`/`impl Tag2 Bool` correctly here, since both goals happen to
-- be satisfiable directly) -- so a precedence regression that only ever
-- ROUTES THROUGH `inst` is invisible to the value alone. What is NOT
-- invisible is the reversed decl-order permutation (section 4): swapping
-- `impl Tag2 Int`/`impl Tag2 Bool` cannot move either value, because neither
-- goal's answer is chosen by `⊑`-minimality over an overlapping pair -- each
-- of the two impls is the UNIQUE match for its own ground goal. A dispatcher
-- that quietly fell back to declaration-order search (rather than genuine
-- `assum`/`inst` resolution) would still pass sections 1-3 here by accident,
-- but the precedence claim itself -- that the SAME predicate at the SAME
-- instantiation is served from the caller's own dict, not by rebuilding one --
-- has no other observable in this corpus.
--
-- OBSERVED: run/build both print 3 then 4. CONFORMANT.
import mix.{mix}

main =
  println (mix (5 : Int))
  println (mix (True : Bool))
